import SwiftUI

enum SuccessArchitectData360QuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "46", question: "A retail customer ingests order data via a CRM Data Stream from Salesforce and a nightly file via the Ingestion API for a legacy POS system. The architect needs the POS file data to land as a queryable Data Model Object with minimal manual field mapping effort, and it must refresh incrementally without re-processing the full file each night. Which approach best satisfies both requirements?", options: [("A", "Configure the Ingestion API stream to write directly to a DMO and set the refresh mode to Full Refresh."), ("B", "Ingest to a Data Lake Object, map it to a custom DMO, and configure the data stream refresh mode as Incremental Refresh with a designated incremental field."), ("C", "Use a Batch Data Transform to load the file directly into the Individual DMO, bypassing DLO staging."), ("D", "Create the data stream as Full Refresh only, since Ingestion API sources don't support incremental refresh.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Ingestion API sources always land first as a Data Lake Object; mapping the DLO to a DMO and setting Incremental Refresh with an incremental field lets only new/changed records process each run, satisfying both the queryability and incremental-load requirements. * A fails because Ingestion API streams cannot write directly to a DMO — they must stage as a DLO first, and Full Refresh reprocesses the entire file every run. * C fails because Batch Transforms operate on already-ingested DLOs/DMOs, not raw ingestion files, and writing directly into the standard Individual DMO bypasses required identity/mapping steps. * D fails on a factual constraint: Ingestion API streams do support Incremental Refresh when an incremental field is designated."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "A subscription business wants a Calculated Insight that recalculates customer lifetime value daily and joins data from two DMOs that are each refreshed on different schedules (one hourly, one daily). Support cases show CI failures occur intermittently right after the hourly DMO's refresh window. What is the most likely architectural cause, and what should the architect change?", options: [("A", "The CI formula exceeds the maximum allowed join complexity; simplify to a single-source CI."), ("B", "The CI is running against a source DMO that is mid-refresh, so schedule the CI to run after both source DMOs' refresh windows complete, with buffer time."), ("C", "Calculated Insights cannot reference DMOs with different refresh cadences; both streams must share one schedule."), ("D", "The CI's calculation logic must be moved into Data Actions to avoid refresh-timing conflicts entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Calculated Insights read from DMOs at run time; if a source DMO is actively refreshing, the CI can pick up a partial or in-error state. Scheduling the CI after both underlying refresh windows finish (with buffer) avoids the race condition. * A misdiagnoses a timing issue as a complexity limit; join complexity isn't described as exceeded here. * C is a fabricated constraint — DMOs with different refresh cadences can coexist as CI sources; that's a normal, supported pattern. * D confuses Data Actions (real-time triggered actions) with the batch calculation engine that computes CIs; it doesn't address the timing conflict."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "A healthcare provider's Identity Resolution ruleset unifies Individuals using Email and Phone match rules with \"OR\" logic. After go-live, unrelated patients sharing a household land-office phone number are being merged into a single unified Individual, creating a compliance risk. Which change best resolves this while preserving legitimate matches on email?", options: [("A", "Remove the Phone match rule entirely and rely on Email matching only."), ("B", "Change the ruleset logic from OR to AND across all match rules."), ("C", "Keep the Phone match rule but add qualifying criteria (e.g., matching last name or date of birth) so phone alone isn't sufficient to trigger a match."), ("D", "Increase the match rule's fuzziness threshold on the Phone field to reduce false positives.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Adding qualifying criteria to the Phone rule preserves its value for genuine matches while preventing a single shared attribute (a household phone) from unifying unrelated individuals — this is standard match-rule design for weak identifiers. * A over-corrects and loses valid matches where phone-based unification is legitimate and email is unavailable. * B breaks matching for individuals who only share one strong identifier (e.g., email but different phones), since AND logic requires every rule to match. * D addresses fuzzy/typo matching, not the real problem, which is that an exact match on a shared household phone is being treated as sufficiently unique on its own."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "An architect configures a reconciliation rule strategy for a unified Individual profile where multiple source systems (CRM, e-commerce, call center) provide conflicting values for the same attribute (e.g., preferred email). The customer wants the call center system's value to always win for phone-related fields, but the e-commerce system's value to win for email fields. Which approach correctly satisfies this?", options: [("A", "Configure a single global \"Most Recently Updated\" reconciliation rule across all fields, since recency always reflects source-system priority."), ("B", "Configure separate reconciliation rules per attribute group, each with its own source-priority order (e.g., call center prioritized for phone fields, e-commerce prioritized for email fields)."), ("C", "Reconciliation rules apply uniformly to the entire Individual object; per-field source prioritization is not supported, so the customer must pick one dominant source for all fields."), ("D", "Use Data Transforms to hard-code the winning source for each field before Identity Resolution runs, since reconciliation rules cannot reference source system identity.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Data 360 supports configuring reconciliation rules at the attribute (or attribute-group) level, each with its own source-priority ordering — this lets different fields defer to different \"winning\" source systems, exactly matching the customer's requirement. * A misapplies recency as a universal proxy for priority; the customer's requirement is source-based, not recency-based, and the two can conflict (e.g., a stale call-center update could still need to win for phone fields). * C is a fabricated platform limitation — per-field/attribute-group reconciliation rules with distinct source priority are a supported, standard capability, not an all-or-nothing choice. * D misassigns the mechanism; Data Transforms operate on data before/outside the unification process and are not the intended tool for expressing source-priority winner logic that IR's reconciliation rules already handle natively."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "A customer disables several fields on a CRM Data Stream, and the next refresh throws a generic Gack error instead of a clear message. Investigation reveals the mapping still references a field that no longer exists on the source object. What should the architect recommend as the standing operational practice to prevent this class of failure?", options: [("A", "Disable stream refreshes automatically whenever any source field is deactivated, platform-wide."), ("B", "Before deactivating or deleting any source field referenced in an active stream mapping, first remove or remap that field in the Data Stream mapping, then deactivate the source field."), ("C", "Rely on the platform to auto-remove orphaned field mappings during the next scheduled refresh."), ("D", "Switch the stream to Full Refresh mode, which re-validates all field mappings automatically before each run.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Data Stream field mappings are not automatically kept in sync with source schema changes; the safe sequencing is to update/remove the mapping first, then deactivate the source field, avoiding a dangling reference that surfaces as an opaque error at refresh time. * A is an overbroad, unsupported behavior — no such platform-wide automatic disablement exists. * C is a factual trap: orphaned mappings are not auto-cleaned; they persist and fail at refresh. * D misunderstands refresh mode: Full vs. Incremental controls how much data is reprocessed, not whether field mappings are validated against the current source schema."),
        Question(id: "51", question: "A Data Stream created from a managed-package Data Kit becomes permanently stuck in Failure status, and the documented workaround (delete and recreate) is blocked because the stream, its DLO, and DMO all have dependencies tied to the Data Kit's own metadata. What is the correct escalation path?", options: [("A", "Manually delete the underlying DMO via Data Loader to force removal, bypassing the UI dependency check."), ("B", "Engage Support to remove the Data Kit-imposed dependencies and coordinate with the Data Kit product team to unlock the components on the backend, then delete/recreate the stream."), ("C", "Uninstall and reinstall the entire managed package, which will automatically clear all component locks."), ("D", "Change the DMO's category from Standard to Custom to remove the Data Kit's ownership lock.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Data Kit-owned components carry backend locks that only Support/Engineering coordination with the product team can safely release before the customer can delete and recreate the stream — this matches the documented resolution path for this exact failure mode. * A is unsafe and unsupported — Data Loader/bulk deletion bypassing dependency checks risks orphaned metadata and further corruption. * C is a disproportionate, risky action that can affect unrelated components and isn't the documented fix for a component-lock issue. * D fabricates a capability; DMO category cannot be changed to bypass ownership locks tied to a managed package."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "Ahead of a production go-live, an architect needs to delete five Data Streams that are no longer needed, but deletion is blocked because each stream references a shared Data Stream Template. What is the most appropriate action to unblock the go-live timeline?", options: [("A", "Delete the Data Stream Template first, which will cascade-delete all dependent streams automatically."), ("B", "Edit each stream to remove its association with the template via the UI, since templates are always detachable."), ("C", "Engage Support/Engineering, since template-level dependencies blocking bulk stream deletion may require backend intervention beyond standard UI actions."), ("D", "Rename the Data Stream Template so the streams no longer resolve to it, effectively unlinking them.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "When deletion is blocked by a structural dependency on a shared template that standard UI actions can't resolve, escalating to Support/Engineering for backend investigation is the correct path, consistent with how this exact scenario required an engineering investigation to unblock. * A is backwards and risky — deleting the template first is not how the dependency resolves, and cascade behavior isn't guaranteed or safe. * B assumes a UI capability that isn't confirmed to exist for template-linked streams in this failure mode. * D is not a supported mechanism; renaming a template does not sever a structural dependency reference."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "A customer's sandbox refresh fails during provisioning with a unique-constraint violation on a backend ingestion job table's primary key. The architect suspects the issue is a metadata design gap rather than a data problem. What is the most likely underlying cause?", options: [("A", "The sandbox has exceeded its DMO count limit, which blocks all provisioning jobs."), ("B", "The affected table's primary key does not include a tenant-scoping identifier, so records from multiple tenants collide during the sandbox metadata export/import."), ("C", "The customer's Ingestion API payloads contain special characters that corrupt the primary key during export."), ("D", "The sandbox template is outdated and needs to be regenerated from the current production Data Kit version.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A unique-key violation on a job-tracking table during multi-tenant metadata export/import points to a primary key that omits a tenant identifier, causing collisions across tenants — this matches the documented root cause for this exact error. * A is an unrelated constraint (DMO count) with no established connection to primary-key collisions on an ingestion job table. * C conflates a different documented issue (Ingestion API special-character handling) with this metadata-export problem. * D is a plausible-sounding but unsupported guess; template staleness isn't the documented cause of a primary-key collision."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "A customer ingesting data via the Ingestion API sees intermittent 403 Forbidden errors traced to special characters in field names and file names. The architect needs a durable, scalable fix rather than a one-off cleanup. Which combination of actions best addresses this long-term?", options: [("A", "Instruct the customer to manually rename every affected file before each upload."), ("B", "Enforce naming conventions restricting object/field/file names to letters, digits, and underscores, and introduce a cleansing/normalization step (e.g., via middleware or a cloud storage buffer) before data reaches the Ingestion API."), ("C", "Increase the Ingestion API's error retry count so transient 403s resolve automatically on their own."), ("D", "Switch entirely to CRM Data Streams, since Ingestion API does not support any non-alphanumeric characters ever.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The durable fix combines governance (naming conventions) with an automated pre-ingestion cleansing/normalization layer, matching the documented set of resolution options (sanitization, naming enforcement, storage-connector buffering, or middleware transformation) rather than relying on manual, one-off intervention. * A doesn't scale and is exactly the ad hoc, error-prone practice the fix is meant to eliminate. * C misdiagnoses the issue: 403 Forbidden is a validation/schema rejection, not a transient failure that retries resolve. * D overstates the constraint and abandons a working ingestion path unnecessarily; the issue is character handling, not a blanket API limitation."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "A customer's ad hoc document ingestion pipeline (individual PDF/image/CSV uploads) shows inconsistent success rates across file types and sizes. Before troubleshooting individual failures further, what should the architect evaluate first?", options: [("A", "Whether increasing the customer's Data Cloud license tier will proportionally increase ingestion success rates."), ("B", "Whether a content-repository-based ingestion approach would be more scalable and consistent than continuing with ad hoc individual file uploads."), ("C", "Whether converting all files to CSV format before ingestion eliminates the inconsistency, since CSV is the only fully supported format."), ("D", "Whether enabling Full Refresh on the ingestion stream will reprocess and correct any previously failed files.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When inconsistency spans multiple file types and sizes in an ad hoc upload pattern, the architectural question is whether the ingestion pattern itself is fit for purpose — evaluating a content-repository-based approach addresses the scalability root cause rather than chasing individual file failures. * A assumes a licensing cause with no evidence; license tier doesn't govern per-file ingestion consistency. * C is a false constraint — Data 360 ingestion supports multiple file types; CSV is not the only viable format. * D confuses refresh mode with root-cause fitness of the ingestion pattern; Full Refresh reprocesses data but doesn't fix an unscalable upload approach."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "The Data Cloud Ingestion API returns intermittent 503 Service Unavailable and Timeout errors across both QA and production simultaneously, with no correlated code or configuration change on the customer side. What is the most appropriate architect response?", options: [("A", "Immediately rebuild the customer's ingestion pipeline with retry-and-backoff logic, assuming the customer's integration is the root cause."), ("B", "Recognize this pattern as likely platform-side/downstream degradation, confirm no data loss via subsequent successful scheduled runs, and track it with the responsible platform engineering team rather than re-architecting the customer's pipeline."), ("C", "Advise the customer to migrate immediately from Ingestion API to CRM Data Streams, since Ingestion API is inherently less reliable."), ("D", "Disable all scheduled ingestion jobs until the customer's development team can rewrite the integration layer.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Simultaneous 503/timeout errors across environments with no customer-side change is a signature of platform-side degradation; the correct response is to confirm no data loss and escalate/track with the platform engineering team, not to assume and fix a customer-side defect that doesn't exist. * A misattributes the root cause to the customer's integration despite no correlated customer-side change. * C draws an unsupported reliability conclusion from a transient platform incident and recommends a disruptive migration that isn't justified. * D is a disproportionate reaction that halts legitimate ingestion for a transient, self-resolving platform issue."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "A Sev-1 incident affects multiple Data 360 customers on certain instances: no data stream jobs can run due to a missing permissions policy at the platform level. As the customer's architect, what should you communicate to the customer while Support/Engineering resolves it?", options: [("A", "The customer's own connector permission sets are misconfigured and need to be rebuilt immediately."), ("B", "This is a platform-wide infrastructure issue affecting the affected instance(s), not a customer configuration problem, and engineering is applying the missing permissions policy directly; no customer-side action is expected to resolve it."), ("C", "The customer should delete and recreate all affected data streams to force a fresh permission check."), ("D", "The customer should disable and re-enable Data 360 entirely to trigger a permissions resync.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When the root cause is a platform-wide missing permissions policy applied at the instance level, the correct customer communication is that it's an infrastructure issue being fixed centrally, not something the customer's own configuration caused or can fix. * A misattributes a platform-level infrastructure gap to customer-side misconfiguration. * C proposes a disruptive workaround that doesn't address a policy missing at the instance level and risks unnecessary rework. * D is a drastic, unsupported action that wouldn't resolve a missing platform policy and would be highly disruptive to re-enable Data 360 entirely."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "An Enterprise Knowledge Connector extraction has failed repeatedly, and the case was reopened after an earlier attempted fix didn't hold. Before escalating further, what information should the architect ensure is gathered to enable root-cause diagnosis?", options: [("A", "Only the connector's authentication credentials, since extraction failures are almost always caused by expired tokens."), ("B", "Org/connection details, run status history, record counts per run, and the connector's configuration settings."), ("C", "The customer's total Data Cloud license consumption, since extraction failures are billing-related."), ("D", "A full export of all DMOs in the org, since the failure could originate anywhere in the data model.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Diagnosing a repeatedly failing connector extraction requires connection-specific context — org/connection details, run history, record counts, and configuration — to identify patterns (e.g., partial failures, specific record types) rather than guessing at a single cause. * A narrows prematurely to one specific cause without evidence, when extraction failures have many possible causes beyond auth. * C misattributes a technical extraction failure to billing/licensing, which is unrelated to connector run failures. * D is disproportionate and unfocused — a full DMO export doesn't target the connector-specific data needed and adds unnecessary noise."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "Records in an Identity Resolution ruleset clearly match the configured criteria on inspection — field values align and example records validate the rule — yet the records still are not unifying in production. The architect has ruled out an obvious configuration mistake. What is the appropriate next step?", options: [("A", "Conclude the match rule is fundamentally broken and rebuild the entire ruleset from scratch."), ("B", "Escalate for backend investigation, since a validated rule configuration that still fails to unify suggests a platform-side processing issue beyond visible configuration."), ("C", "Increase the match rule's confidence threshold, since higher thresholds always improve unification rates."), ("D", "Delete the Identity Resolution ruleset and rely solely on the Data Graph API for real-time deduplication instead.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When configuration and example records validate correctly but production unification still fails, the fault likely lies in backend processing rather than visible configuration, warranting escalation for deeper investigation rather than further configuration changes. * A is premature and wasteful — rebuilding a ruleset that has already been validated as correctly configured doesn't address a suspected backend issue. * C is a plausible-sounding but backwards fix: raising confidence thresholds typically reduces matches, worsening a unification shortfall rather than fixing it. * D misapplies the Data Graph API, a query/serving mechanism, as a substitute for Identity Resolution's unification process, which is a different capability."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "An identical Identity Resolution configuration works correctly in one org but fails to unify records in another org with similar data volume. The architect has ruled out record volume as a cause. Which two areas should be investigated next as the most likely sources of the discrepancy? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "DMO/DLO mapping differences between the two orgs (e.g., a field mapped differently or missing)"), ("B", "The Salesforce org's Storage Used percentage, since IR pauses automatically above 80% storage utilization"), ("C", "Identifier data quality differences (e.g., completeness or format of matching fields) between the two orgs"), ("D", "Whether the second org's Salesforce Edition supports Identity Resolution, since IR is Enterprise Edition-only"), ("E", "Org-specific platform behavior or backend state that isn't visible in the ruleset configuration itself"), ("F", "The number of Chatter groups configured in each org, since IR indexes against Chatter-linked records")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "(Note: E is also a reasonable real-world consideration per the source case, but A and C are the two areas an architect can directly investigate and act on.) Since the ruleset configuration itself is identical and volume is ruled out, the most actionable differences are in the underlying data model mapping and the quality/completeness of the identifiers being matched. * B invents a nonexistent storage-based IR throttling mechanism. * D fabricates an edition-gating constraint; Identity Resolution isn't restricted by Salesforce CRM Edition in this way. * F invents an irrelevant dependency on Chatter groups that has no basis in how Identity Resolution processes records."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "A customer builds a custom Identity Resolution approach using the Party Identification object to link an external \"Contacts\" DMO to Salesforce Contacts via a foreign-key field, because no direct join exists between the two DMOs. After go-live, zero records consolidate. What is the most likely root cause category?", options: [("A", "Identity Resolution does not support any custom, non-direct-join configurations, so the approach itself is invalid."), ("B", "A misconfiguration in the Party Identification relationship setup linking the two DMOs, rather than a fundamental limitation of the approach."), ("C", "The external \"Contacts\" DMO must first be converted into a standard Individual DMO before any IR ruleset can reference it."), ("D", "Zero consolidation always indicates the DLO-to-DMO mapping refresh has not yet run, and rerunning the mapping will resolve it automatically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Using Party Identification to bridge a foreign-key relationship between DMOs is a supported, documented design pattern; a zero-consolidation result points to a misconfiguration in that relationship setup, which is resolved through careful review against best practices rather than abandoning the approach. * A incorrectly claims the architecture pattern itself is unsupported, when it is a valid, documented approach for indirect joins. * C invents an unnecessary conversion requirement; DMOs do not need to become the standard Individual DMO to participate in a Party Identification-based IR design. * D oversimplifies to a single mechanical cause; zero consolidation from a relationship misconfiguration won't be fixed merely by rerunning a mapping refresh."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "A learner following a standard Data 360 setup exercise sees an Identity Resolution error stating required fields aren't mapped for the Contact\\_Home stream, and a Calculated Insight built on top fails because its source object is in an error state. What is the correct sequence to resolve this dependency chain?", options: [("A", "Delete the Calculated Insight only, since IR errors do not need to be fixed if the CI is removed."), ("B", "Map the required field (e.g., Phone to Formatted E164 Phone Number) in the stream mapping, refresh the stream, rerun the IR ruleset, then republish the Calculated Insight."), ("C", "Republish the Calculated Insight first to force the platform to auto-map any missing IR fields retroactively."), ("D", "Rebuild the Calculated Insight using a different source DMO that bypasses Identity Resolution entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The dependency chain runs stream mapping → Identity Resolution → Calculated Insight, so fixes must be applied in that order: correct the field mapping, refresh the stream, rerun IR, then republish the CI so it reads a healthy source. * A only removes the symptom's visible consumer without fixing the underlying mapping/IR error, and doesn't restore correct unified profiles. * C reverses the dependency direction; publishing a CI cannot retroactively fix a missing field mapping upstream in IR. * D avoids the actual fix and abandons the unified-profile approach the exercise is meant to demonstrate."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "Using the Data Graph Query API, an architect observes that segment membership information for the Individual DMO (Individual \\- Latest) only appears in the Data Graph after an Identity Resolution run completes, with higher credit consumption noted during those runs. What should the architect factor into design guidance for the customer?", options: [("A", "Segment membership data can be refreshed independently of IR by simply re-querying the Data Graph API on a tighter polling interval."), ("B", "Segment membership materialization for the Individual DMO in the Data Graph is dependent on Identity Resolution runs, so IR scheduling frequency directly affects how current segment membership appears in the Data Graph, with associated credit cost implications."), ("C", "Data Graphs never reflect segment membership under any configuration, so this must be surfaced through a separate Calculated Insight instead."), ("D", "Credit consumption during IR runs is unrelated to Data Graph queries and can be disregarded when planning IR schedules.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Because segment membership only materializes after IR runs (per the documented behavior), the architect must design IR scheduling with both data-freshness needs and credit-consumption cost in mind — the two are linked, not independent. * A is factually wrong per the documented behavior; simply polling more often doesn't materialize membership without an IR run. * C overstates the limitation — the documented issue is a dependency on IR, not a total absence of segment membership support in Data Graphs. * D ignores a real cost trade-off that directly affects IR scheduling decisions and should not be disregarded."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "A long-tenured customer has chronic Data 360 issues: production deployments performed via unsupported file-based metadata changes (rather than managed Data Kits) have corrupted org integrity, foundational mapping errors persist, and Identity Resolution consolidation sits at only 3–7%. As the architect, what is the most appropriate strategic recommendation?", options: [("A", "Provide another round of advisory documentation and best-practice links, since the customer's team simply needs more self-service guidance."), ("B", "Recommend hands-on Professional Services engagement to rebuild the CI/CD deployment pipeline using managed Data Kits, fix the data foundation/mappings, and directly execute the Identity Resolution and Calculated Insight work."), ("C", "Recommend the customer switch entirely to Ingestion API-based deployment to avoid Data Kit dependency issues going forward."), ("D", "Recommend disabling Identity Resolution entirely until the customer's data hygiene improves organically over time.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When chronic, compounding issues stem from unsupported deployment practices and deep data-foundation problems, advisory guidance alone has already proven insufficient — hands-on Professional Services to rebuild the pipeline and directly execute the fixes is the appropriate escalation, matching the documented resolution path for this exact Red Account scenario. * A repeats an approach already shown to be inadequate for a customer at this level of chronic risk. * C misdiagnoses the root cause as an ingestion mechanism choice, when the actual problem is unsupported deployment methodology, not the Data Kit approach itself. * D abandons the identity resolution capability rather than fixing the underlying data foundation causing the low consolidation rate."),
        Question(id: "65", question: "An architect is designing Identity Resolution for a B2B customer where Contacts must unify at the Individual level, but the customer also needs Account-level entity resolution across multiple source systems where account names vary (e.g., \"Acme Inc.\" vs \"ACME Incorporated\"). Which statement correctly describes how to achieve both in Data 360?", options: [("A", "Use one Identity Resolution ruleset configured to unify both Individuals and Party (Account-like) records simultaneously, since IR supports multi-entity rulesets by default."), ("B", "Configure a separate Identity Resolution ruleset scoped to the Individual DMO for Contacts, and use a separate matching approach (e.g., fuzzy match rules) scoped to the Party Identification/Account-equivalent object, since each IR ruleset resolves to a single unified object."), ("C", "Rely on Einstein Duplicate Rules imported from Sales Cloud, since Data 360 IR does not support fuzzy name matching."), ("D", "Build a single Calculated Insight that flags likely duplicate account names, since Data 360 does not support entity resolution for non-Individual objects.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Each Identity Resolution ruleset in Data 360 resolves to one unified DMO; unifying both Individuals and an Account-like entity requires two distinct rulesets, each scoped and tuned (including fuzzy match rules) for its own object. * A violates the platform constraint that a single IR ruleset targets one unification object, not multiple entity types at once. * C is incorrect — Data 360 IR does support fuzzy matching (e.g., Fuzzy Match methods) and doesn't require importing Sales Cloud duplicate rules. * D is factually wrong; Data 360 does support match/reconciliation rules against non-Individual DMOs such as Party Identification-based entities."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "A marketing team needs to activate a segment of 2 million high-value customers to Marketing Cloud Engagement nightly, but also needs a real-time trigger to fire a Data Action the moment a customer's cart value crosses a threshold during a browsing session. Which combination of Data 360 capabilities should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Use a single batch Segment with a daily schedule for both use cases, since Data Actions can only be triggered from batch segment membership changes."), ("B", "Use a batch Segment activated on a nightly schedule for the MCE audience, and a separate Data Action triggered by a streaming/real-time Data Transform or Calculated Insight condition for the cart-threshold event."), ("C", "Use Data Cloud Flow Triggers exclusively, since Segments cannot activate to Marketing Cloud Engagement."), ("D", "Configure the segment's activation frequency to \"Real-Time\" so one segment handles both the nightly MCE sync and the instant cart-value trigger.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Batch segmentation with a scheduled activation is the right tool for a recurring, large-scale audience sync to MCE, while a real-time/streaming trigger (via a streaming Data Transform or condition on a Calculated Insight) is needed to react instantly to an in-session event like a cart-value threshold. * A is wrong because Data Actions can also be triggered by real-time/streaming conditions, not only batch segment changes — collapsing both use cases into one daily batch segment would make the cart trigger a day late. * C incorrectly claims Segments can't activate to MCE — that is a standard, supported activation target. * D is a governance/mechanism trap: segment activation frequency does not have a \"Real-Time\" mode that also satisfies a nightly batch sync; real-time responsiveness and scheduled batch activation are different mechanisms serving different needs."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "A financial services customer requires that PII fields (SSN, account number) ingested into Data 360 be masked for most internal users but visible in full to a small compliance team. The architect also needs Row-Level Security so that regional analysts only see records from their own region. Which combination correctly implements both requirements?", options: [("A", "Apply Data Space separation by region and rely on Data Space isolation alone to also mask PII, since Data Spaces control both row and field visibility."), ("B", "Apply Field-Level Security via masking/tokenization policies on the PII fields, and separately configure Row-Level Security (RLS) rules scoped by region to control record-level visibility."), ("C", "Use Salesforce Shield Platform Encryption on the underlying DLOs, since Data 360 does not support field-level masking natively."), ("D", "Rely on Permission Sets alone to hide the PII fields, since Data 360 objects inherit CRM field-level security automatically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Data 360 separates these concerns: masking/tokenization policies handle field-level PII protection, while Row-Level Security rules (typically driven by a security predicate on a DMO) independently control which records a user can see — together they satisfy both requirements without conflating them. * A misapplies Data Spaces: they partition data for organizational/tenant separation, not field-level PII masking, so relying on them alone leaves PII unmasked within a space. * C is a deprecated/legacy-approach trap: Shield Platform Encryption applies to Salesforce CRM objects, not Data 360's DLOs/DMOs, so it wouldn't cover this scenario's ingested data. * D is a governance/licensing trap: standard CRM permission sets do not automatically extend to Data Cloud DMOs, which have their own security model."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "A telecom wants to trigger a Next Best Action flow in Agentforce the moment a Calculated Insight flags a customer as \"high churn risk,\" using near-real-time data rather than waiting for the next batch cycle. Which two components should the architect combine to meet this requirement? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "A Data Action configured with a Calculated Insight-based trigger condition"), ("B", "A batch Data Transform scheduled hourly to approximate real-time behavior"), ("C", "A streaming Data Transform feeding the source DMOs used by the Calculated Insight so it reflects near-real-time signals"), ("D", "An Einstein Prediction Builder model deployed directly against the DLO layer"), ("E", "A Flow or Agentforce action subscribed to the Data Action's outbound event"), ("F", "A Segment scheduled to refresh every 15 minutes as the sole trigger mechanism")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 4], explanation: "A Data Action with a trigger condition tied to the Calculated Insight is the mechanism that detects the flagged state, and a Flow/Agentforce action subscribed to that Data Action's outbound event is what actually fires the Next Best Action — together they form the real-time trigger-to-action chain. * B fails because hourly batch scheduling is not real-time and defeats the stated \"near-real-time\" requirement — it's a right-outcome/wrong-mechanism trap. * C alone doesn't trigger anything; it only improves data freshness upstream of the CI, so it doesn't satisfy the triggering requirement by itself. * D misapplies Einstein Prediction Builder, which operates on DMOs/CRM data for scoring, not as a real-time trigger mechanism against raw DLOs. * F is a scope-limit trap: segment refresh scheduling is still batch-based, and segments aren't designed as the sole real-time trigger for individual customer events."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "A nonprofit customer using NPSP wants to bring Fundraising objects into Data 360 via a CRM Data Stream. All relevant profiles have object-level Read and View All access, yet the data stream setup screen still reports the Fundraising objects as unavailable. What is the most likely root cause and correct fix?", options: [("A", "The Data Cloud license doesn't support NPSP data at all; the customer must use the Ingestion API instead."), ("B", "The hidden Data Cloud integration user is missing the license-gated system permission (e.g., \"Fundraising User\") on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set — grant that permission directly on the connector permission set."), ("C", "The customer needs to assign the NPSP Permission Set License directly to the Data Cloud integration user."), ("D", "The CRM Data Stream feature does not support managed-package objects; a custom Ingestion API stream must be built instead.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "License-gated industry objects require the specific system permission enabled directly on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set used by the hidden integration user — object-level Read/View-All alone isn't sufficient because the gating permission is separate from standard object security. * A is a factual trap; Data 360 does support NPSP/Fundraising objects via CRM Data Streams once permissions are correctly configured. * C violates a licensing constraint: the integration user cannot hold Permission Set Licenses, so assigning a PSL to it has no effect. * D is a deprecated/wrong-approach trap — CRM Data Streams do support managed-package objects; the issue here is permissions, not stream type."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "After months of stable operation, a production DMO stops being recognized by the Data 360 query engine — queries referencing it fail with \"DMO not found,\" even though the DMO's definition looks correct in Data Model setup. No recent deployments or field changes were made. What is the recommended first troubleshooting step before escalating?", options: [("A", "Delete and recreate the DMO from scratch, since \"not found\" errors always indicate a corrupted definition."), ("B", "Open and re-save the DMO (even without changes) to trigger a metadata cache refresh, then retest the query."), ("C", "Rebuild the entire data model package via a new Data Kit deployment to force a full metadata resync."), ("D", "Escalate directly to Engineering, since query engine metadata issues cannot be resolved by an admin.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "A stale metadata cache is a known, low-risk cause of \"DMO not found\" errors; re-saving the DMO forces a metadata cache refresh without any destructive change, and is the appropriate first step before deeper investigation. * A is an overreaction that risks data loss for a problem that a simple re-save often fixes. * C misapplies Data Kit deployment, a packaging/deployment mechanism, to a metadata cache problem it isn't designed to solve directly. * D skips a simple, safe self-service step that resolves this exact symptom in documented cases, before escalation is warranted."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "A retailer's segment of 9 million loyalty members experiences activation publishing delays. Investigation shows delays correlate with concurrent activation jobs writing to a shared, curated Loyalty DMO that has data skew from repeated identifier values. Which two actions should the architect recommend to address the root cause? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Reduce the segment size to under 1 million members so activation always completes within the batch window"), ("B", "Stagger or serialize concurrent activation jobs that write against the shared Loyalty DMO to avoid write-lock contention"), ("C", "Work with the data engineering team to address the data skew (repeated identifier values) in the curated Loyalty DMO"), ("D", "Switch the segment's activation target from Marketing Cloud Engagement to the Ingestion API to bypass locking"), ("E", "Increase the segment's refresh frequency to hourly so smaller batches reduce contention"), ("F", "Convert the DMO from a curated DMO to a Data Graph, since Data Graphs are not subject to write locks")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [1, 2], explanation: "The documented root cause was write-lock contention from concurrent jobs plus data skew on the shared DMO; staggering/serializing the concurrent writes and fixing the underlying identifier skew both directly address that mechanism. * A is a padded, ineffective distractor — segment size was explicitly ruled out as the cause here. * D misunderstands activation targets; the Ingestion API is for inbound ingestion, not an alternative activation destination. * E would likely worsen contention by increasing the frequency of concurrent writes against the same shared DMO. * F is an invalid-mechanism trap: Data Graphs are a query/serving layer over DMOs, not a replacement for a curated DMO."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "A logistics company has four Azure-backed Data Streams, each mapped to a different DMO, all connected through a single shared Azure connector. Streams intermittently fail with vague \"Backfill failed job stats\" errors whenever two or more refresh schedules overlap. What is the best long-term architectural fix, beyond simply staggering the schedules?", options: [("A", "Consolidate all four DMOs into a single DMO so only one stream is needed."), ("B", "Provision separate Azure connectors per DMO (or serialize sync requests) so concurrent refreshes don't compete for one connector's single non-terminal sync task slot."), ("C", "Switch all four streams to the Ingestion API, since file-based ingestion doesn't have connector task limits."), ("D", "Increase the Azure connector's polling frequency so tasks complete faster and avoid overlap.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The root cause is that a single connector permits only one non-terminal sync task at a time; giving each DMO its own connector (or serializing requests) removes the structural bottleneck rather than just avoiding it via scheduling. * A is a right-outcome/wrong-mechanism trap — merging DMOs changes the data model to work around a connector limitation. * C swaps one ingestion mechanism for another without evidence it's appropriate for this Azure-sourced case, and doesn't resolve the described root cause. * D misunderstands the constraint: it's a concurrency limit on non-terminal tasks, not a throughput/polling-speed issue."),
        Question(id: "73", question: "A global retailer operating under GDPR and CCPA needs its Data 360 implementation to respect consent preferences captured in multiple source systems before any segment can activate a customer to an outbound marketing channel. Which two capabilities should the architect leverage? (Choose 2\\)", options: [("A", "Contact Point-level consent management with unified consent status flowing into segmentation criteria"), ("B", "Data Space isolation as the sole mechanism for enforcing consent, since separating by region satisfies GDPR/CCPA"), ("C", "Segment exclusion/suppression logic that filters out individuals lacking valid opt-in consent for the given channel before activation"), ("D", "Relying on the destination platform (e.g., MCE) to independently filter unconsented contacts post-activation"), ("E", "Einstein Trust Layer configuration, since it governs consent for generative AI prompts rather than marketing consent"), ("F", "A single global consent flag on the Individual DMO that ignores channel-specific or source-system differences")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 2], explanation: "Consent should be modeled at the Contact Point/channel level and unified across sources, then actively used as suppression/exclusion criteria in segmentation before activation — this ensures unconsented individuals are never sent downstream. * B misapplies Data Spaces, a tenant/regional data-partitioning mechanism, as a substitute for consent enforcement. * D is a governance risk trap: deferring consent enforcement to the destination platform means unconsented data has already left Data 360\\. * E is an adjacent-product trap: the Einstein Trust Layer governs AI/LLM data usage and grounding, not marketing consent management. * F oversimplifies a real requirement — channel-specific consent (e.g., opted into email but not SMS) is standard and a single flag can't represent it."),
        Question(id: "74", question: "A customer's DLO-to-DMO mapping fails intermittently, with no consistent error pattern across attempts. Before escalating, which sequence of self-service checks should the architect have the customer perform?", options: [("A", "Immediately delete and recreate the DMO, since intermittent mapping failures always indicate a corrupted DMO definition."), ("B", "Retry the mapping (since it's often transient), check for null targetField values among the mapped fields, and try resyncing the DMO by opening and re-saving it without changes."), ("C", "Escalate directly to Engineering without any self-service attempt, since mapping failures are always backend platform bugs."), ("D", "Switch the ingestion method from a CRM Data Stream to the Ingestion API, since mapping failures only occur with CRM-sourced DLOs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The documented low-risk self-service sequence — retry, check for null targetField values, and resync via re-save — resolves the majority of intermittent mapping failures without destructive action or unnecessary escalation. * A is a disproportionate, risky first step for an intermittent (not consistently reproducible) failure. * C skips reasonable self-service diagnostics that resolve this class of issue in documented cases. * D assumes an ingestion-method-specific cause with no evidence, and abandons a working CRM Data Stream unnecessarily."),
        Question(id: "75", question: "A large customer's org has grown to roughly 5,700 DMOs. Suddenly, all Data Model tooling — DMO creation, Relationships, Calculated Insights, mapping, and Segments — begins throwing a generic internal server error across the entire org, with no recent deployments or package installs identified as a trigger. What should the architect suspect and investigate first?", options: [("A", "A corrupted individual DMO is cascading errors to all other Data Model features; isolate and delete the most recently created DMO."), ("B", "Possible breach of an org-wide DMO count limit or threshold that is causing all Data Model operations to fail, warranting investigation with Support/Engineering."), ("C", "The customer's Salesforce Edition has changed and no longer supports the Data Model feature set."), ("D", "A missing Calculated Insight dependency is blocking all unrelated Data Model tooling from loading.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When every Data Model feature fails simultaneously at a very high DMO count with no deployment trigger, a count-limit/threshold breach is the leading suspected cause to investigate, consistent with the documented case at this scale. * A incorrectly assumes a single-DMO root cause for an org-wide, cross-feature failure pattern. * C invents an edition-change explanation with no supporting evidence in the scenario. * D misattributes an org-wide tooling failure to one specific dependency, which wouldn't explain failures across unrelated features like DMO creation and Relationships."),
        Question(id: "76", question: "A healthcare customer's Data Kit deployment triggers cascading failures across Calculated Insights, Data Graphs, Data Transforms, and Identity Resolution because some existing objects lack required mappings and certain DLOs aren't available in the target org, with one blocking DLO itself stuck in an error state and undeletable. What is the most appropriate resolution approach?", options: [("A", "Force-delete the stuck DLO using a mass-delete data management tool to clear the blockage immediately."), ("B", "Open an engineering investigation and engage the relevant Data Streams product/scrum team to resolve the stuck DLO and missing mappings before retrying the Data Kit deployment."), ("C", "Abandon the managed Data Kit approach entirely and manually rebuild every dependent object (CIs, Data Graphs, Transforms, IR) from scratch."), ("D", "Reduce the Data Kit's scope by removing all Identity Resolution components, since IR is the most likely source of the cascading failure.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When a DLO is stuck in an error state and can't be deleted through normal means, and multiple downstream features are cascading-failing from missing mappings/DLOs, engineering engagement with the responsible product team is the correct path — matching the documented resolution for this exact multi-feature cascading failure. * A is unsafe; force-deleting a stuck DLO outside supported tooling risks further corruption and doesn't address the missing-mapping root cause. * C is disproportionate and abandons the intended benefit of managed Data Kits (consistency, supportability) over a targeted, resolvable blocking issue. * D misdiagnoses the cause; the described failures span multiple features (CIs, Data Graphs, Transforms), not specifically IR, so removing IR wouldn't resolve the DLO/mapping blockage."),
        Question(id: "77", question: "An architect discovers that a specific external DLO can no longer be mapped to the Individual DMO — any new field mapping or mapping update on that pairing throws \"Unable to save, please try again,\" while all other DLO/DMO pairings work fine. Investigation reveals a DMO interface field (e.g., a boolean-like flag) was originally created with the wrong data type (Number instead of Text), a defect since fixed for new DMOs in a later platform release but not retroactively. What is the correct remediation?", options: [("A", "Wait for the next platform release, since the fix will automatically retroactively correct existing mistyped fields."), ("B", "Delete and recreate the mistyped field on the affected DMO with the correct data type, then retry the mapping."), ("C", "Create a new DMO from scratch with all fields recreated, since a single field's data type cannot be changed once set."), ("D", "Change the DLO's field data type instead of the DMO's, since the DLO is always the source of truth for type mismatches.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Since the platform fix only prevents the mismatch on new DMOs and doesn't retroactively correct existing ones, the documented remediation is to delete and recreate the specific mistyped field on the affected DMO, which resolves the mapping failure without a full DMO rebuild. * A is factually wrong — the fix is explicitly non-retroactive for existing DMOs, so waiting does not resolve it. * C is an overcorrection; only the single mistyped field needs to be fixed, not the entire DMO. * D misdiagnoses which side needs correction; the documented root cause is the DMO interface field's type, not the DLO's."),
        Question(id: "78", question: "A customer complains that Data Processing Engine (DPE) only surfaces Calculated Insight validation errors when the entire flow is saved, rather than incrementally as each transformation node is added, making debugging difficult. What architecture pattern should the architect recommend to reduce this troubleshooting pain going forward?", options: [("A", "Perform all transformation logic directly inside DPE nodes and avoid pre-aggregating data upstream, so errors surface as early as possible in the flow."), ("B", "Do heavy transformation work upfront using a Batch Transform into a DLO, map that DLO to a DMO, then load into the CRM object with one object load per definition — minimizing reliance on DPE's own transformation troubleshooting."), ("C", "Split the single DPE flow into as many separate flows as there are nodes, since smaller flows always validate incrementally."), ("D", "Disable save-time validation in DPE settings so errors only surface at runtime instead, giving more flexibility during flow design.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Moving heavy transformation work upstream (Batch Transform → DLO → DMO → CRM object) reduces dependence on DPE's own error surfacing, since the transformation logic is validated and resolved before ever reaching DPE — this is the documented recommended pattern for this exact pain point. * A does the opposite of the recommended pattern, increasing reliance on DPE's own transformation logic and its late error surfacing. * C is a fabricated claim; splitting flows doesn't inherently change when DPE validates Calculated Insight errors. * D removes a safety check rather than addressing the root workflow problem, and doesn't reduce debugging pain — it just moves error discovery even later, to runtime."),
        Question(id: "79", question: "A Marketing Cloud Next customer's Scoring Model publish fails, and the associated Data Graph throws \"doesn't contain valid personalization information.\" Following documented guidance, the customer adds the Email Address field from Contact Point Email to a new Data Graph, but the error persists. What should the architect investigate next?", options: [("A", "Assume the Data Graph feature itself is broken for Marketing Cloud Next customers and file a product defect immediately."), ("B", "Check whether an underlying Data Stream or DMO that the Data Graph depends on (e.g., a Party Identification-related object) is itself in a failed/error state, since adding a field won't fix a broken upstream dependency."), ("C", "Remove the Email Address field again, since adding personalization fields to a Data Graph is never the correct fix for this error."), ("D", "Rebuild the Scoring Model from scratch, since Scoring Model publish failures are unrelated to Data Graph configuration.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When the standard documented fix (adding the personalization field) doesn't resolve the error, the next step is to check whether an upstream dependency — a source Data Stream or DMO the Data Graph relies on — is itself in an error state, since a Data Graph can't surface valid personalization data from a broken source. * A jumps to a product-defect conclusion without first ruling out a known root-cause category (upstream source errors) documented for this exact symptom. * C contradicts the documented guidance, which does call for adding the personalization field as a first step — the issue here is that a deeper cause remains even after doing so. * D disconnects two related systems; the Data Graph error is explicitly tied to the Scoring Model publish failure in this scenario, not an unrelated issue."),
        Question(id: "80", question: "A System Administrator with full permissions and the ability to query all included DMOs via the Query Editor still sees \"You can't view or create this Data Graph because you don't have access to all objects and fields\" when opening the Data Graph builder. What should the architect suspect, and what is the recommended first remediation step?", options: [("A", "A genuine governance/permissions gap exists despite admin status, since Data Graphs enforce a separate, stricter permission model than the Query Editor; grant additional field-level security explicitly."), ("B", "This is likely a backend/metadata state issue rather than a true access problem (a pattern seen in prior sandbox cases); try \"Update Status\" and \"Retry\" from the Data Graph UI before escalating."), ("C", "The System Administrator profile must be replaced with a custom profile that explicitly lists every DMO and field referenced by the Data Graph."), ("D", "Data Graphs require a dedicated Data Graph License separate from the Data Cloud license; verify the license is provisioned.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Since a System Admin already has full access and can query all included DMOs successfully, a real permissions gap is ruled out; the documented pattern is a backend/metadata state issue, and the recommended first step is \"Update Status\"/\"Retry\" from the UI before escalating further. * A incorrectly asserts a stricter separate permission model exists for Data Graphs that would explain this error for a full System Admin, contradicted by the admin's proven query access. * C is an unnecessary, disruptive overcorrection when the real issue is not standard object/field security. * D invents a licensing requirement not indicated anywhere in the scenario or documented pattern."),
        Question(id: "81", question: "A new Realtime Data Graph in a non-production/test environment gets stuck in a \"Creating\" state indefinitely and never completes, a recurring pattern the architect has seen before in similar environments. What is the correct escalation sequence?", options: [("A", "Immediately escalate to the Data Graph secondary on-call without attempting any self-service step first."), ("B", "Attempt \"Update Status\" and \"Retry\" from the Data Graph UI first; if unresolved, escalate to the Data Graph secondary on-call for backend investigation."), ("C", "Delete the Realtime Data Graph and recreate it as a standard (non-Realtime) Data Graph, since Realtime Data Graphs are not supported in non-prod environments."), ("D", "Wait 24–48 hours without action, since \"Creating\" state issues always self-resolve given enough time.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The documented standard recovery sequence for a stuck \"Creating\" state is to try \"Update Status\" and \"Retry\" from the UI first, escalating to the Data Graph secondary on-call only if that doesn't resolve it — balancing self-service speed with appropriate escalation. * A skips a low-risk, often-effective self-service step before consuming on-call escalation resources. * C invents an unsupported environment restriction; Realtime Data Graphs are not documented as prohibited in non-prod environments. * D assumes automatic self-resolution with no supporting evidence, delaying a fix unnecessarily."),
        Question(id: "82", question: "A production Data Graph cannot be deleted, and the architect sees no obviously visible dependencies in the UI. Based on documented patterns for this exact symptom, what should be checked before escalating, and what is the less common alternate cause?", options: [("A", "Check for remaining dependencies (linked content, activations, or segments still referencing the graph) first; the less common alternate cause is an internal API error where UI-based deletion succeeds but API-based deletion fails."), ("B", "Check whether the Data Graph exceeds a row-count limit, since Data Graphs above a certain size cannot be deleted through any interface."), ("C", "Check whether the customer's Data Cloud contract has expired, since expired contracts lock all Data Graphs against deletion as a data-retention safeguard."), ("D", "Check whether the Data Graph was created via a managed package, since only packaged Data Graphs carry hidden dependencies.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The documented pattern for this exact symptom is that deletion blocks usually come from remaining dependencies (activations, segments, linked content) not visible at first glance, with a less common alternate cause being a UI/API deletion-path discrepancy — both should be checked before escalating. * B fabricates a row-count-based deletion restriction with no basis in the documented pattern. * C invents a contract-expiration mechanism unrelated to the actual documented causes (dependencies or a UI/API discrepancy). * D incorrectly restricts the dependency-blocking behavior to only packaged Data Graphs, when the documented cases don't distinguish by creation method."),
        Question(id: "83", question: "Multiple CRM Data Stream refreshes across an org begin failing, all citing missing fields or objects, even though the relevant profiles and the customer's own admin access look correct. The architect suspects the hidden Platform Integration User lost a specific permission set. What is the correct diagnostic and remediation sequence?", options: [("A", "Query for the permission set (e.g., \"Data 360 Salesforce Connector\" or its legacy-named equivalent) by label, confirm it's missing from the Platform Integration User, and reassign it via a PermissionSetAssignment insert."), ("B", "Reset the customer admin's password, since CRM Data Stream failures citing missing fields are almost always caused by expired admin credentials."), ("C", "Recreate all affected CRM Data Streams from scratch, since stream-level corruption is the most common cause of missing field/object errors."), ("D", "Grant \"Modify All Data\" to every profile in the org, since broad object permissions bypass any missing-permission-set issue.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "The documented recurring root cause for broad CRM stream failures citing missing fields/objects is the hidden Platform Integration User losing its connector permission set; querying for the permission set and reassigning it via an anonymous Apex insert is the correct, targeted fix. * B misattributes the cause to the customer admin's credentials, when the failing user context is the hidden Platform Integration User, not the admin. * C is a disproportionate action that doesn't address a permission-set assignment issue and risks unnecessary rework. * D is an overbroad, insecure workaround that doesn't specifically address the missing connector permission set and violates least-privilege practice."),
        Question(id: "84", question: "An NPSP customer has full object-level Read/View-All access granted on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set for Fundraising objects, and the objects are fully queryable in Salesforce, yet Data 360 still reports \"Object unavailable\" when creating a data stream. What is the root cause and fix?", options: [("A", "The hidden Data Cloud integration user needs the specific license-gated system permission (e.g., \"Fundraising User\") enabled directly on the connector permission set, since industry Permission Set Licenses assigned to the integration user aren't sufficient (it can't hold PSLs)."), ("B", "Fundraising objects are entirely unsupported by Data 360 CRM Data Streams and must be ingested via the Ingestion API instead."), ("C", "The customer must purchase an additional Data Cloud add-on SKU specifically for NPSP object support."), ("D", "Object-level Read/View-All access needs to be granted at the record level instead, since Fundraising objects use record-level security exclusively.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "License-gated industry features require their specific system permission enabled directly on the connector permission set used by the hidden integration user; because that user cannot hold Permission Set Licenses, object-level access alone (even with View-All) is insufficient without this extra step. * B is a factual trap; Fundraising objects are supported via CRM Data Streams once the correct permission is enabled — no need to switch ingestion methods. * C invents a licensing/SKU requirement not indicated in the documented root cause. * D misdirects to record-level security, which isn't the described gating mechanism — the issue is a missing system permission on the connector permission set."),
        Question(id: "85", question: "A customer in a regulated financial-services vertical can't see the FinancialDeal object when creating a CRM data stream, despite having reviewed and confirmed standard object permissions. Based on a documented similar pattern (e.g., Fundraising), what should the architect check?", options: [("A", "Whether the object needs to be globally enabled via a Salesforce Trust status page setting, unrelated to permission sets."), ("B", "Whether the specific industry system permission (e.g., \"FSC Deal Management Platform User\") is enabled on the Data Cloud Salesforce Connector permission set used by the hidden integration user."), ("C", "Whether the customer's org language/locale setting is blocking the object from appearing in stream creation."), ("D", "Whether the object needs to first be exposed via a custom Lightning page before Data 360 can detect it.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "This mirrors the documented Fundraising pattern: license-gated industry objects require their specific system permission enabled directly on the connector permission set for the hidden integration user, not just standard object-level security. * A invents an unrelated trust/status-page mechanism with no basis in the documented pattern. * C misattributes the cause to locale/language settings, which have no documented connection to object visibility in stream creation. * D fabricates a UI-exposure prerequisite that isn't part of how CRM Data Stream object availability works."),
        Question(id: "86", question: "An FSC Data Kit deployment repeatedly fails on several relationship objects with SFDC\\_PERMISSION\\_ERROR, even after the architect confirms via direct SOQL that the connector permission set has full read/view-all access on all flagged objects, restarts Core, waits out possible cache TTLs, and runs a tenant metadata sync Black Tab action. What should the architect conclude and do next?", options: [("A", "Conclude the permission set itself must be recreated from scratch, since a permission set that has been verified correct once can still silently corrupt."), ("B", "Conclude the standard permission-set fix has been ruled out via direct verification, and escalate to the core/off-core data-services team for the specific sync mechanism needed to resolve a deeper permission-check/cache-propagation gap."), ("C", "Conclude the FSC Data Kit itself is defective for this object set and recommend the customer skip those objects entirely."), ("D", "Conclude the customer's sandbox is unsupported for FSC Data Kits and recommend deploying directly to production instead.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "When the standard permission-set fix has been directly verified as correct via SOQL, and Core restarts, cache TTL waits, and tenant metadata sync haven't resolved it, the documented next step is escalating to the core/off-core data-services team for a deeper sync mechanism — the issue lies beyond standard permission-set configuration. * A is unsupported speculation about silent corruption with no evidence, given the permission set was directly verified as correctly configured. * C abandons required functionality without addressing the actual root cause, which is a deeper platform sync gap, not a Data Kit defect. * D recommends deploying to production to work around an unresolved permission/sync issue, which is inappropriate risk management, not a fix."),
        Question(id: "87", question: "A sandbox can't enable Data 360 because the org requires deleting an existing Agentforce Data Library first, but that Data Library shows no visible records in the UI — a known pattern of orphaned backend metadata not visible in the UI. What is the suspected fix path?", options: [("A", "Recreate the Agentforce Data Library with the same name so the system recognizes it as already deleted."), ("B", "Enable a Support-only org permission (e.g., SfdcInternalQAClients) via a Black Tab action to allow cleanup of the orphaned metadata."), ("C", "Have the customer manually edit the org's metadata XML via Workbench to remove the Data Library reference."), ("D", "Disable and re-enable the entire sandbox to force a metadata resync that clears orphaned records automatically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The documented suspected fix for this exact orphaned-metadata pattern is enabling a Support-only internal org permission via a Black Tab action, which allows backend cleanup of metadata that isn't visible or manageable through the standard UI. * A doesn't address orphaned backend metadata; recreating a UI-visible library with the same name won't resolve a backend-only artifact. * C is an unsupported, risky action — manually editing org metadata via Workbench for backend-only artifacts isn't the documented or safe approach. * D is a drastic, unsupported action with no indication that a full sandbox disable/re-enable clears this specific orphaned metadata issue."),
        Question(id: "88", question: "A customer setting up Tableau Next reports the \"Load Data\" step for bringing standard objects into Data 360 getting stuck at 0% indefinitely, even after stopping/restarting the process and toggling Tableau Next off and on. What is the appropriate next step for the architect?", options: [("A", "Conclude this is expected behavior, since \"Load Data\" always takes 24+ hours for standard objects regardless of org size."), ("B", "Escalate to the relevant swarm channel to check for a known issue, since standard self-service remediation (restart, toggle off/on) has already been attempted without success."), ("C", "Recommend abandoning Tableau Next and using a manual CRM Data Stream for every standard object instead."), ("D", "Recommend the customer purchase additional Data Cloud storage capacity, since \"Load Data\" stalls are typically a storage capacity issue.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Since the documented self-service steps (restart, toggle off/on) were already tried without success, escalating to the swarm channel to check for a known issue is the appropriate next step, consistent with how this exact case was handled. * A fabricates an expected-duration claim with no support; the case describes this as a stuck/failed state, not a long-but-normal process. * C is a disproportionate architectural change in response to what may be a known, fixable platform issue. * D invents a storage-capacity root cause with no evidence connecting it to this symptom."),
        Question(id: "89", question: "A customer repeatedly fails to install the C360 Semantic Model app during the \"Load Data\" step, even after reinstalling from Platform Tools. The architect suspects the install flow may not be checking for the latest app version. What should be verified first?", options: [("A", "Whether the \"Load Data\" flow is actually referencing and installing the latest available version of the C360 Semantic Model app, rather than a stale cached version."), ("B", "Whether the customer has enough API call allocation remaining for the day, since app installs consume API calls exclusively."), ("C", "Whether the customer's Salesforce org is on Lightning Experience, since Semantic Model apps require Lightning exclusively."), ("D", "Whether the customer's timezone setting is causing the install job to schedule incorrectly.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Given the architect's stated suspicion and the documented open question in this case, verifying whether the install flow is actually pulling the latest app version (versus a stale reference) is the direct next diagnostic step for a persistent \"Load Data\" install failure. * B invents an API-allocation cause with no connection to app installation version-checking behavior. * C fabricates a Lightning Experience requirement not indicated in the scenario. * D invents a timezone-based scheduling cause unrelated to an app-version install failure."),
        Question(id: "90", question: "Salesforce discovers that a subset of GovCloud customers were provisioned into a FedRAMP-authorized Data 360 instance not intended for GovCloud Civilian use, though the instance was never accessed and no customer data was impacted. What is the correct customer-facing remediation process?", options: [("A", "Silently migrate the affected orgs' data in the background with no customer notification, since no data impact occurred."), ("B", "Notify affected customers by email, and in Data Cloud Setup under Data Cloud Lifecycle Management → Clean Up Your Org, have them review deletion details, check the consent box, and click \"I Approve Data Deletion\" to trigger reprovisioning into the correct instance."), ("C", "Leave the customers on the current instance indefinitely, since FedRAMP authorization exceeds GovCloud Civilian requirements and downgrading isn't necessary."), ("D", "Require the customers to open a new support case to manually request reprovisioning, since no self-service reprovisioning path exists for GovCloud orgs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "The documented remediation explicitly required customer notification and a customer-driven, consent-based self-service flow (Clean Up Your Org → I Approve Data Deletion) to trigger reprovisioning into the correct instance — compliance/instance-assignment issues require explicit customer consent before data deletion and reprovisioning. * A violates transparency and consent requirements; customers must be notified and must actively consent to the deletion/reprovisioning step. * C misunderstands the compliance requirement — GovCloud Civilian customers must be on the instance designated for that specific compliance scope, regardless of the other instance's authorization level. * D contradicts the documented process, which is a self-service Setup flow, not a support-case-driven manual request."),
